  "title": "Why doesn’t this electric bell ring just once? #Shorts",
  "description": "A classic self-interrupting electromagnetic bell: holding the button supplies the coil, the armature moves and opens its own contact, current and magnetic pull decay, and the spring returns the armature to reclose the circuit. A battery supplies every cycle. This is an illustrative lumped model, not a measured commercial bell. Simplified translating armature, linear unsaturated magnetic material, ideal hysteretic contact and impact restitution. Open-contact inductive energy dissipates through an effective transient path; arc/contact-bounce details and full 3D field effects are omitted. Time is expanded and replayed for explanation; field lines are schematic.\n\nMusic:\nMonkeys Spinning Monkeys — Kevin MacLeod (incompetech.com)\nLicensed under Creative Commons Attribution 3.0\nhttps://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\nMusic excerpt trimmed, faded and mixed beneath narration.\n\n\nPhysics references: OpenStax College Physics — magnetic fields, RL circuits and Hooke’s law.\nhttps://github.com/openstax/osbooks-college-physics-bundle/blob/main/modules/m42382/index.cnxml\nhttps://github.com/openstax/osbooks-college-physics-bundle/blob/main/modules/m42425/index.cnxml\nhttps://github.com/openstax/osbooks-college-physics-bundle/blob/main/modules/m42240/index.cnxml",
